lesson.body:
You have already seen overriding happen in passing, in the Square and Dog examples. This section names the pattern directly, because it is one of the most important tools inheritance gives you. Overriding means a child class provides its own implementation of a method that its parent already defines, using the exact same method name. When that method is called on an instance of the child, Python runs the child's version, not the parent's.

It helps to be precise about what this is not. Python does not support traditional method overloading, where you would define several methods with the same name but different parameter lists and let the language pick the right one. Overriding is different: it is one class replacing another class's method entirely, based on which class the object actually belongs to, not based on the arguments passed in.

```python
class Notifier:
    def send(self, message):
        return f"Generic notification: {message}"

class EmailNotifier(Notifier):
    def send(self, message):
        return f"Emailing: {message}"

class SMSNotifier(Notifier):
    def send(self, message):
        return f"Texting: {message}"

notifiers = [Notifier(), EmailNotifier(), SMSNotifier()]
for n in notifiers:
    print(n.send("Server is down"))
```

This prints three lines: `Generic notification: Server is down`, `Emailing: Server is down`, and `Texting: Server is down`. All three objects are asked to do the same thing, call `.send("Server is down")`, with the exact same method name and the exact same argument. Yet each one responds differently, because each class overrides `send` with its own logic. Python decides which version to run by checking the actual class of the object first, `EmailNotifier` or `SMSNotifier`, before ever falling back to `Notifier`.

Note that overriding does not delete the parent's version, it just takes priority for that class and its instances. As you saw with `super()` in the previous section, a child can still reach the parent's original method deliberately by calling `super().send(message)`, if it wants to build on top of the parent's behavior rather than fully replacing it. This loop over a list of different notifier objects, all called through the exact same method name, is also a preview of the next section's topic: polymorphism, the ability to treat objects of different but related classes uniformly, letting each one respond in its own way.
